var __index = {"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"]},"docs":[{"location":"index.html","title":"Welcome","text":"<p>Welcome to my workshop (or toy box) for productivity and fun.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#categories","title":"Categories","text":"<ul> <li>Experience</li> </ul> <p>The personal insights based on what I have observed and learned. I try to summarize the best practices from everyday life.</p> <ul> <li>Product</li> </ul> <p>Recommendations for tools that can be used for specific purposes, or toys generated from practice. These are intended for study or fun, with no commercial use.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#why-write","title":"Why Write","text":"<p>Writing makes people profound, as Francis Bacon famously said. Any information that enters the mind is initially a subjective feeling, and then it is scattered in the form of words within our mind. To describe the feeling is equivalent to experiencing it again, with the key difference being that by reorganizing it into objective words, the information is restructured and output in a more organized way. In the process, the information becomes more diversified and inspires creative thought. Additionally, by sharing one's opinion with others, any feedback may help the author reduce blind spots.</p> <p>I had the idea to write my personal electronic notes some years ago. Constrained by the busyness of work, I always delayed doing so, until two key factors were met:</p> <ol> <li>As GPT matured enough and became freely usable, I could use it as my reviewer to help me be more confident in the shared content.</li> <li>I came across some fine examples. In particular, I briefly browsed The Blue Book, with its wide variety of content and well-organized, one-stop navigation. It amazed me and motivated me to take some free time to write my personal notes.</li> </ol> <p>Tip</p> <p>When browsing any online website, any third party site should always be considered untrusted. Before navigating to an external site, ensure your personal information remains secure. Jim's Docs and its author and maintainer are not responsible for privacy and security on third party sites. If you encounter any confusing or suspicious content, consider verifying it using a tool like The Internet Archive's Wayback Machine.</p>"},{"location":"experience/hybrid-app-approach.html","title":"Hybrid App Approach","text":"<p>A good essay clarifying the concept of hybrid apps can be found in Native vs. Cross-Platform Apps.</p> <p>The simplest definition of a hybrid app is an app that depends on an embedded browser for functionality. However, this definition is overly simplistic. Below, I propose some more detailed perspectives.</p>"},{"location":"experience/hybrid-app-approach.html#ui-performance","title":"UI Performance","text":"<p>When discussing native apps, my most familiar platform is macOS, where I use Swift, Cocoa, and SwiftUI for app development.</p> <p>In theory, hybrid apps tend to lag behind native apps in performance due to the intermediate layer between native components and the web container. However, real-world performance often diverges from theory. The flexibility of front-end development can enable hybrid apps to mitigate these performance gaps, while native apps may not always follow best practices.</p> <p>Consider the simplest text control as an example. I created a document-based app in Xcode using two different UI components: NSTextView (via Storyboard) and Text (via SwiftUI). To test their performance, I opened a large single-line text file (100 MB) and resized the app window, scroll inside to evaluate text-wrapping speed. The intuitive feeling is, the Text component performed worse than the NSTextView component.</p> <p>This discrepancy arises because SwiftUI exposes more context during view calculations, making it easier for developers to introduce resource-intensive operations inadvertently. By contrast, NSTextView provides more optimization interfaces, such as NSTextStorage and NSTextContainer, which empower developers to fine-tune performance.</p> <p>For comparison, Apple's TextEdit, an ancient app, offers excellent performance for opening large single-line text files, serving as a benchmark. Another tool, VSCode, can open large text files almost as quickly while providing advanced features like line numbering and a minimap.</p>"},{"location":"experience/hybrid-app-approach.html#development-efficiency","title":"Development Efficiency","text":"<p>Thanks to the active front-end ecosystem, hybrid apps provide many productivity-enhancing features, such as Hot Module Replacement (or HMR). Even when testing an app hosted on a remote server, the speed of hybrid app development often surpasses that of native tools.</p>"},{"location":"experience/hybrid-app-approach.html#communication-performance","title":"Communication Performance","text":"<p>The backend process of a web view can migrate to the native program, offering an alternative to web workers for time-consuming tasks with simple input/output requirements. Additionally, tasks involving permission-sensitive operations can be invoked natively and triggered by the web view. However, this raises a critical design challenge: how to structure communication between the web view and the native context effectively.</p> <p>Data Transfer Performance - Sending messages from the native app to the web view Initially, I implemented data transfer by composing JavaScript code as serialized strings and injecting it into the web view via <code>WKWebView.evaluateJS()</code>. While straightforward, this approach required escaping numerous characters when constructing JavaScript code, making it error-prone.</p> <p>Later, I practiced the WKUIDelegate  delegate method invoked when <code>window.prompt</code> is called in the web view. This method proved to be more efficient than the initial implementation and introduced me to the concept of a \"JSBridge.\"</p> <ul> <li>Sending messages from the web view to the native app At first, I used a callback function as the <code>webview.evaluateJS()</code> argument. This callback could receive evaluation results and errors, accommodating various data types like strings and dictionaries. While this method is simple and accessible, it has a notable limitation: the web view cannot actively send messages to the native app.</li> </ul> <p>Eventually, I learned about the web view message handler API, which enables the web view to post messages. By registering a message handler in the web view, this approach streamlined communication and improved efficiency.</p>"},{"location":"experience/hybrid-app-approach.html#maintainability","title":"Maintainability","text":"<p>The interaction between native code and web components functions like a bridge. As the only coupling point between the native and web layers, this bridge often requires updates whenever new capabilities are added to the web view component. To improve maintainability, modularizing the web view bridge is crucial.</p> <p>One effective strategy I have practiced the JSON-RPC-based bridge. JSON-RPC, widely used in browser-server communication, offers a structured and consistent protocol for message exchange. For instance, the Language Server Protocol (LSP) adopts a JSON-RPC approach, demonstrating its ability to scale across diverse use cases. Its simplicity and adherence to widely understood standards make it an approach that is both accessible and easily accepted.</p>"},{"location":"experience/hybrid-app-approach.html#usability","title":"Usability","text":"<p>When designing a tool or feature, it's essential to consider its return on investment (or ROI). Resolving this simple question ensures clarity and inspires purposeful development. The guiding principle should be that every return outweighs its investment. Start with a simple demonstration, and refine it into a robust, scalable module.</p> <p>For web view components, consider these three use cases:</p> <ol> <li>Standalone Browser Support: Provide injected data to enable standalone web page demos, speeding up development and testing.</li> <li>Native App Integration: Ensure seamless control and display within a native app.</li> <li>Other Extensions: Adapt the component for integration with VSCode or Chrome, leveraging their safe environments and design practices.</li> </ol> <p>Regardless of the approach, asynchronous web view loading is common ground. It's essential to design mechanisms that deliver consistent performance across use cases.</p> <p>To maximize adaptability, implement context recognition on the web side rather than the native side, enabling the web component to integrate seamlessly across different environments.</p>"},{"location":"view/client-status.html","title":"Client Status","text":"<p>Show current client information.</p>  Quick Look"},{"location":"view/diagram.html","title":"Diagram","text":"<p>Here are some libraries related to the diagram view:</p> <ul> <li>alibaba/butterfly</li> <li>antvis/g6<ul> <li>Rare rendering method. Combines Canvas and HTML and SVG by rendering HTML DOM or SVG DOM above the Canvas DOM</li> </ul> </li> <li>antvis/x6</li> <li>bpmn-io/bpmn-js<ul> <li>Online Editor</li> </ul> </li> <li>bramp/js-sequence-diagrams</li> <li>cytoscape/cytoscape.js</li> <li>eclipse-sprotty/sprotty</li> <li>jgraph/drawio<ul> <li>SVG-based rendering</li> <li>Draw.io Tools</li> <li>Online Editor</li> </ul> </li> <li>jgraph/mxgraph<ul> <li>Online Editor</li> </ul> </li> <li>mermaid-js/mermaid<ul> <li>Online Demo</li> </ul> </li> <li>NorthwoodsSoftware/GoJS<ul> <li>Canvas-based rendering</li> </ul> </li> <li>open-source-labs/Svelvet</li> <li>svgdotjs/svg.js</li> <li>xyflow/xyflow<ul> <li>HTML-based rendering</li> <li>reactflow</li> <li>svelteflow</li> </ul> </li> </ul> <p>Some tools can serve as excellent benchmarks:</p> <ul> <li>Microsoft Visio</li> <li>Windmill Hub</li> <li>yFiles<ul> <li>Support both SVG Rendering and Canvas Rendering</li> </ul> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"view/markdown.html","title":"Markdown","text":"<p>Here are some inspired related libraries:</p> <ul> <li>laurent22/joplin</li> </ul>"},{"location":"view/markdown.html#custom-tool","title":"Custom Tool","text":"<p>A tool to edit markdown text and view result.</p>"},{"location":"view/markdown.html#prerequisite","title":"Prerequisite","text":"<ul> <li>Browser: chrome &gt;= 87</li> </ul>"},{"location":"view/markdown.html#live-demo","title":"Live Demo","text":"Quick Look (click to show)"},{"location":"view/rich-text.html","title":"Rich-Text","text":"<p>The following libraries are useful for building or integrating rich-text editors:</p> <ul> <li>TypeCellOS/BlockNote</li> <li>ueberdosis/tiptap</li> </ul>"},{"location":"view/rich-text.html#simple-example","title":"Simple Example","text":"<p>You can enable a basic browser-native rich-text editor using the <code>contenteditable</code> attribute:</p>  Quick Look (click to show)"},{"location":"view/table.html","title":"Table","text":"<p>Here are some great libraries that can be used for building a table:</p> <ul> <li>apache/openoffice</li> <li>audreyt/ethercalc</li> <li>cryptpad/cryptpad</li> <li>Calligra Suite (or KOffice)</li> <li>dream-num/Luckysheet<ul> <li>A powerful canvas base spreadsheet-like library.<ul> <li>dream-num/univer</li> <li>Refactor project of <code>Luckysheet</code></li> </ul> </li> </ul> </li> <li>Gnumeric</li> <li>handsontable/handsontable</li> <li>jspreadsheet/ce</li> <li>libreoffice</li> <li>onlyoffice</li> <li>SheetJS/sheetjs<ul> <li>\ud83d\udcd7 SheetJS Spreadsheet Data Toolkit</li> <li>Related Recommendations<ul> <li>adazzle/react-data-grid</li> <li>glideapps/glide-data-grid</li> <li>olifolkerd/tabulator</li> <li>TonyGermaneri/canvas-datagrid</li> </ul> </li> </ul> </li> <li>TanStack/table<ul> <li>A highly customizable and performant table library.</li> </ul> </li> <li>VisActor/VTable</li> </ul> <p>Some closed-source tools can serve as excellent benchmarks:</p> <ul> <li>Google Sheets</li> <li>Microsoft 365 Online Excel</li> <li>softmaker office</li> <li>SpreadJS</li> </ul>"},{"location":"view/whiteboard.html","title":"Whiteboard","text":"<p>Below are some libraries that may be useful for implementing the whiteboard:</p> <ul> <li>excalidraw/excalidraw</li> <li>tldraw/tldraw</li> </ul>"}]}